      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 30 mg is three times the FDA-labeled maximum for amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. At that dose, the risks include severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and marked peripheral edema. Overdose can also cause prolonged shock that responds poorly to treatment. Normal kidney and liver function and a weight of 70 kg don't change that ceiling.\n\nCould this be a typo or a unit mix-up? Here are some options:\n\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO daily**, the usual maximum.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO daily**, a common starting dose, then titrate.\n- **Another agent added to amlodipine**, if you need more BP control than 10 mg gives. Examples are an ACE inhibitor or ARB, or a thiazide-like diuretic.\n\nIf you do want 30 mg daily, tell me and say it's intentional. I can enter it with a note recording that, though pharmacy will probably want to verify it. Otherwise, tell me which dose to enter."
      }
     ],
